d I have no B7 or B9 to build upward, **B8** seems least useful. Also, B8 appears 4 times unshown \u2014 still available, but not urgent.  \n- **W2** is tied to seat wind (West Wind = W), so a **Seat Wind** pair/meld would give 2 points. But I only have one W2, and no second.  \n- **J1**, **J2**, **J3**: holding all three dragons gives flexibility, but unless I can pair one, they\u2019re just singles.  \n- Comparing all, **T1** is the weakest connector: no chow potential without T2/T3 (I have T1/T2, but no T3), and T3 is low (only 3 left), not enough to bank on.  \n- **Play T1** removes a marginal tile and keeps honor/dragon options open.\n\nReason:  \nT1 has limited potential for forming meaningful chows or melds. While I have T2, I lack T3 or T4 to complete a sequence easily, and the previous player already has a Chow involving T3-T4-T5. Discarding T1 avoids blocking progress toward more viable patterns like Knitted Straight or Half Flush. Other tiles like B5, B6, B8 support possible future chows (e.g., B5-B6-B7), and honors (J1, J2, J3) offer scoring potential via dragon-related patterns. Thus, T1 is the safest and most logical discard.\n\nAnswer: Play T1"}
