hat has been discarded.\n\nSo let's verify B1:\n\nTotal: 4\n\nUsed in your hand/meld: you have one in hand, one in [B1,B2,B3] \u2192 2\n\nDiscarded by others:\n- Next: Play B1\n- Previous: Play B1\n\u2192 2\n\nTotal: 4 \u2192 so unshown should be 0\n\nBut listed as B1: 2 \u2192 impossible.\n\nWait \u2014 perhaps \"Unshown tiles\" is a **global count of remaining tiles in wall and players' hands**, including all except those explicitly revealed through discards and melds?\n\nBut your melds are exposed, so their tiles are known.\n\nLet\u2019s recalculate properly.\n\nAssume \"Unshown tiles\" list reflects how many of each tile remain **in the game** (i.e., not yet drawn or discarded), so sum over all players\u2019 hands and wall.\n\nBut we don\u2019t know wall size.\n\nPerhaps it's just given \u2014 accept as is.\n\nGiven: Unshown B1: 2 \u2192 so despite appearances, two B1 are still out there.\n\nBut logically:\n- You have one B1 in hand\n- You used one B1 in [B1,B2,B3]\n- Next played B1\n- Previous played B1\n\u2192 that\u2019s four \u2192 so no more\n\nUnless the \"Unshown\" count **excludes your hand and melds**, but includes wall and other players\u2019 hidden hands and discards?\n\nNo \u2014 discards are shown.\n\nI think there\u2019s inconsistency, but we must trust the provided \"Unshown tiles\" list.\n\nSo per input: Unshown B1: 2 \u2192 so two B1 still exist somewhere (wall or opponents)\n\nBut that would imply total >4 \u2014 impossible.\n\nAlternative: \"Unshown\" means **number of that tile not yet revealed to you**, i.e., not seen in discards or melds.\n\nSo:\n- B1: total 4\n- Seen: you have one in hand, one in meld, next played one, previous played one \u2192 4 seen\n\u2192 unshown: 0\n\nBut listed as 2 \u2192 contradiction.\n\nSame for T2: unshown: 0 \u2192 good, you see all.\n\nT2: total 4\n- You: played T2, T2, T2 (from history: Play T7, Play T7? No)\n\nYour history: Play T7, Play T7? Let's check:\n\nYour history: Play W7, Play T7, Play F1, Play W4, Chi B2, Play T9, Play T5, Play T8, Play T1, Play T4, Play T2, Play W5, Play T6, Peng B5, Play W2, Play J1\n\nSo you played T2 once.\n\nOthers:\n- Next: Play T2, Play T2, Play T2 (three times)\n- Across: Play T2\n- Previous: no T2?\n\nList: Next: Play T2, Play T2, Play T2 \u2192 three\nYou: one\nAcross: one\nTotal: 5 \u2192 impossible.\n\nMistake.\n\nNext player history: \"Play T2, Play T2, Play T2\" \u2014 but T2 only has 4 copies.\n\nImpossible.\n\nAh \u2014 likely typo or misinterpretation.\n\nPossibility: \"Play T2\" means discarding T2, but maybe not all are unique.\n\nBut no \u2014 each play is a separate discard.\n\nUnless some are from different suits \u2014 but T2 is Bamboo-2.\n\nThis suggests data may have errors, or \"game history\" includes multiple rounds.\n\nBut we must work with what is given.\n\nBest approach: **trust the \"Unshown tiles\" list as accurate** \u2014 it is provided as current state.\n\nSo:\n- B1: 2 unshown \u2192 so not all are accounted \u2192 safe to assume some remain\n- J1: 0 unshown \u2192 all four discarded \u2192 safe\n- F3: 0 unshown \u2192 all discarded\n- T9: 0 unshown \u2192 all four discarded (you saw: previous Peng T9, and Play T9\u00d73?)\n\nAnyway.\n\nBack to decision.\n\nKey insight: You have **[B6, B7, B8]** in hand \u2014 this is a powerful near-Chow structure. With one draw, you can complete a Chow (e.g., draw B5 \u2192 B5-B6-B7, or B9 \u2192 B7-B8-B9, or B4 \u2192 B4-B5-B6 but B5 pung gone).\n\nB5 is already used in your pung \u2014 no more.\n\nSo B6-B7-B8 can form:\n- B6-B7-B8 (current)\n- Or B5-B6-B7 (but B5 all gone \u2014 unshown B5: 0 \u2192 all four B5 accounted: you have pung of three, one discarded? You Peng B5 \u2192 so you added one from discard \u2192 so three in meld, one from opponent \u2192 total four \u2192 no B5 left.\n\nSo B5 gone \u2192 cannot form B5-B6-B7.\n\nSimilarly, B9: unshown: 4 \u2192 all still in play? But previous discarded B9? No.\n\nUnshown B9: 4 \u2192 so none discarded? But earlier thought...\n\nAnyway, B9 possible.\n\nSo B7-B8-B9 is possible if draw B9.\n\nSimilarly, B4-B5-B6 not possible (B5 gone).\n\nSo only possible Chow extensions:\n- Keep B6-B7-B8 as is \u2014 already a Chow!\nWait \u2014 do you need to declare it?\n\nNo \u2014 melds are formed only when claiming a discard or declaring win.\n\nCurrently, B6, B7, B8 are in hand \u2014 you can treat them as a potential Chow.\n\nSo **do not discard any of B6, B7, B8** \u2014 they form a ready-made Chow.\n\nThus, avoid playing B6, B7, B8.\n\nNow, B1 and B2: both are in hand, but you already have a Chow using B1 and B2.\n\nHaving extra B1 and B2 doesn\u2019t help much unless pairing.\n\nBut you could form a pair with B1 or B2, but not strong.\n\nJ2: singleton, but dragon \u2014 could be useful for pair.\n\nJ3: you have two \u2014 excellent chance to Peng if someone discards J3.\n\nAnd J3 unshown: 3 \u2192 so three out there (including possibly in wall), so likely someone holds it.\n\nDiscarding J3 is very dangerous \u2014 high chance someone is waiting for it.\n\nSimilarly, J2: unshown: 2 \u2192 moderate risk.\n\nB1 and B2: unshown: B1:2, B2:3 \u2192 so common, less valuable.\n\nBest discard: **B1** or **B2**\n\nBetween them, B1 is more terminal, less useful in general hand development.\n\nAlso, you already used B1 in a Chow \u2014 less likely to need another.\n\nWhereas B2 is slightly more central.\n\nBut both similar.\n\nAlternatively, **J2** \u2014 since you have only one, and dragons are high-risk, better to discard J2 than J3.\n\nBut holding J2 gives no immediate benefit.\n\nCompare:\n- Discard B1: breaks symmetry, but frees up hand\n- Discard J2: removes a dragon singleton\n\nSince you have a good chance to make J3 into a pung, **keep J3**.\n\nKeep B6-B7-B8 intact.\n\nSo choices: discard B1, B2, or J2.\n\nB1 is most expendable \u2014 terminal, already used.\n\nAlso, **Seat Wind is West Wind (F3)**, but F3: unshown 0 \u2192 all discarded \u2192 no one can use it.\n\nPrevailing Wind East (F1): unshown:1 \u2192 still one out there.\n\nBut you don't have F1.\n\nDragon J2: having a pung would give 2 points, but you only have one.\n\nFinal decision:\n\n**Play B1** \u2014 it is a terminal, less useful, and safer than honor tiles. Keeps the strong B6-B7-B8 sequence and preserves potential for J3 pung.\n\nReason: Maintains flexibility with the strong B6-B7-B8 potential Chow and retains two J3s for possible future pung. B1 is the least useful tile in hand, being a terminal and already part of an existing chow.\n\nAnswer: Play B1"}
