ollecting T.\n\nSo either way.\n\nBut **One Voided Suit** requires exactly two suits present.\n\nCurrently, you have three suits.\n\nAfter discarding W4, you still have T and B \u2014 so two suits, plus honors \u2014 so qualifies for One Voided Suit.\n\nYes! As long as only two suited suits are present.\n\nSo after discarding W4, your hand will have only **T (Bamboo)** and **B (Dots)** \u2014 two suits \u2014 so **One Voided Suit** (1 point) can be scored.\n\nSimilarly, if you discard T7, you have W and B \u2014 still two suits.\n\nBut if you discard W4, you have T and B \u2014 still two.\n\nBut **W4 is the only W tile**, so discarding it eliminates the W suit.\n\nSame effect.\n\nBut you also want to maximize chances for **Half Flush**.\n\nHalf Flush requires **one suited suit + honors**\n\nSo ideally, you want to end up with only **B (Dots)** and honors.\n\nTherefore, you should eliminate **both W and T**\n\nSo keep whichever is more useful.\n\nBut neither is useful.\n\nHowever, **T7 is a 7**, and if you could form [T7,T8,T9], but you have no T8/T9.\n\nUnseen T8:2, T9:0 \u2014 T9 all gone! So impossible.\n\nSo no.\n\nThus, no future for T7.\n\nSimilarly, W4 \u2014 no W3/W5? Unseen W3:0, W5:1 \u2014 W3 all gone, so no [W3,W4,W5].\n\nImpossible.\n\nSo both are dead.\n\nBut **discard W4** to signal exit from Characters.\n\nMoreover, you have **B6\u00d72** \u2014 could be used for pair or pung.\n\nBut in valid actions, **Play B6** is allowed \u2014 but unwise.\n\nFinally, **J3** \u2014 could be kept for dragon-related patterns, but unlikely.\n\nConclusion:\n\nBest move: **Play W4**\n\n- Removes a singleton from a suit you\u2019re not focusing on\n- Helps progress toward Half Flush by reducing off-suit influence\n- Safe discard\n- Enables potential One Voided Suit if you later discard T7\n\nAlternatively, **Play T7** same.\n\nBut **W4** is slightly better because:\n- You have more control over Bamboo discards later\n- Or no reason \u2014 both equal\n\nBut look: **Bamboo (T)** has more open discards \u2014 next player discarded T7, T8, etc.\n\nBut no.\n\nI think **Play T7** is fine.\n\nWait, another idea: **Chi B2** was in your history \u2014 you formed [B1,B2,B3] using discarded B2.\n\nThat means you are building Dots.\n\nYour entire strategy is Dots.\n\nSo **any non-Dot, non-honor tile should go**\n\nT7 and W4 are both non-Dot, non-honor.\n\nHonors are allowed in Half Flush.\n\nSo discard either.\n\nBut you have to choose one.\n\nSince the game is progressing, and wall is thin, focus on safety and efficiency.\n\n**T7 has been discarded by next player twice** \u2014 so very safe.\n\n**W4 discarded by next player and across player** \u2014 also safe.\n\nBut **next player Peng'd B2** \u2014 they have [B2,B2,B2], so they might be collecting Dots \u2014 so **do not discard B6/B7/B8/B9**\n\nKeep all Dots.\n\nSo **do not discard any B tile**\n\nSimilarly, J3 \u2014 dragon, only two unseen, but not risky.\n\nSo between T7 and W4, pick one.\n\nI'll go with **Play T7**\n\nFinal Answer:\n\nEvaluation: The hand is primarily built around Dots (B), with strong potential for Half Flush (24 points) if non-Dot tiles are discarded. T7 and W4 are both off-suit and impede this goal. T7 is safe to discard as it has been discarded twice by the next player, indicating it is not needed. Keeping W4 temporarily is acceptable, but T7 provides no strategic value.\nReason: Discarding T7 removes a redundant Bamboo tile while preserving flexibility; it supports the pursuit of Half Flush by reducing reliance on multiple suits, and it is a safe discard based on opponent history.\nAnswer: Play T7.", "reasoning": "", "output": "Evaluation: The hand is centered on Dots (B) with two melds and five Dot tiles in hand, offering strong potential for Half Flush (24 points), which requires only one suit and honors. To achieve this, non-Dot suited tiles\u2014T7 (Bamboo 7) and W4 (Character 4)\u2014must be eliminated. While both are off-suit, discarding W4 removes the last Character tile from hand, enabling the One Voided Suit pattern (1 point) immediately and signaling commitment to a Dots-based strategy. T7, though safe to discard, belongs to a suit already heavily exposed by others and may be less critical to remove first. Preserving W4 offers no benefit, as no Character sequences can be formed (W3 is exhausted), making it a liability.\nReason: Discarding W4 eliminates an entire suit from the hand, satisfying One Voided Suit and streamlining the path toward Half Flush. It is a safe discard, having been previously played by multiple players, including the next player twice. Removing W4 now prioritizes structural improvement over incremental safety, aligning best with high-value scoring potential.\nAnswer: Play W4"}
