12-point pattern). Slight value in keeping.\n\nYour current hand: [W5, B6, B6, B7, B8, B9, F2, J3] + melds [[B1,B2,B3],[B5,B5,B5]]  \nYou\u2019re close to **Full Flush** (all Bams/Dots and honors), except for W5 and F2. Removing W5 and F2 improves that chance.  \nAlso possible: **Upper Tiles** (if you go for B7-B9 focus), **Half Flush**, or even **All Simples** if you shift (but B1/B5/B9 break that).\n\nBest direction: Transition toward **Half Flush** (Bamboos + Honors) or **Outside Hand**, using existing honor tiles.  \nKeep useful high Bamboos (B7,B8,B9) for potential chows or pungs.  \nB6 is redundant and safer to discard than B7/B8/B9.\n\nReason:  \nDiscarding **B6** maintains flexibility while removing a duplicate middle tile that doesn\u2019t strongly contribute to high-scoring patterns. It avoids feeding opponents' potential chows compared to B7/B8/B9. W5 and F2 should go eventually, but W5 is safer now; F2 is too dangerous to keep because the next player already has a pung of F2. However, **F2 cannot be played** as it would gift a win \u2014 so among valid *safe* options, **B6** is optimal.\n\nAnswer: Play B6"}
